Class field visibility is dropped in the parser: ClassMember::Field holds a VarDecl with no is_private, so no layer above can filter private fields


## Reported by lane-resolver, 2026-08-31, while auditing B114

## The defect

`ClassMember::Field` holds a `VarDecl`, and `VarDecl` has **no `is_private`
field at all**. The visibility keyword is parsed and then dropped on the floor.

This is **one layer earlier than B114**. B114 is about modifiers being lost at
*pooling* — the parser had them and the symbol table did not keep them. Here the
parser never records it, so there is nothing for pooling to lose. **B114 cannot
fix this and landing B114 will not.** Fixing it needs an AST change.

## Consequence

Completion filtering is half-blind: it cannot exclude private fields from
member-completion outside the declaring class, at any layer above the parser,
because the information does not exist above the parser. Methods are unaffected
(their visibility survives); it is specifically fields.

## Generalise the tests, per repo policy

A fix here should not stop at `private`. Cover, and write a case for each:

- `private` field, `protected` field, and a field with no keyword (default
  public) — all three, so the default is pinned rather than assumed.
- The same three on a `class`, and on any other member-bearing declaration the
  parser accepts them on.
- Access from inside the declaring class, from a derived class, and from an
  unrelated scope — `protected` is only distinguishable from `private` at the
  derived-class site, so without that row the two keywords are indistinguishable
  and a fix that conflates them passes.
- A field and a method with the SAME visibility keyword in one class, since the
  method path already works and makes a natural in-file control.

## Watch for

Whether the parser currently *rejects*, *silently accepts*, or *errors* on the
keyword before deciding what "add the field" means. Establish that first — the
red test differs in each case, and this batch has already been bitten by writing
a red against an assumed baseline.